<para>begining of period to search with</para></parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">time_end</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>end of period to search with</para></parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">timelist</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>ascending time values to search between</para></parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="out">index2</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>indices of bounding time values within Timelist</para></parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">correct_leap_year_inconsistency</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>When true turns on a kluge for an inconsistency which may occur in a special case. When the modulo time period (i.e. Time_end - Time_beg) is a whole number of years and is not a multiple of 4, and the calendar in use has leap years, then it is likely that the interpolation will involve mapping a common year onto a leap year. In this case it is often desirable, but not absolutely necessary, to use data for Feb 28 of the leap year when it is mapped onto a common year. </para></parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
</para>        </detaileddescription>
